{"capabilities":[{"name":"package_tests","status":"probe_confirmed","check_id":"6d278d47122b403eaa290de33064114c","description":"Package build and selected existing tests; not a full suite or scheduling probe"},{"name":"precise_schedule_replay","status":"unavailable","check_id":null,"description":"No pre-established deterministic asynchronous or crash schedule control; generated experiments must demonstrate prerequisites"}],"parameters":{},"remaining_seconds":1993.6110884460013,"directed_question":null,"snapshot_id":"982925d88e4d4cacae0d6aacab69fc68","harness_kind":"go_test","harness_instructions":"Use package raft and TestAssurance-prefixed Go tests. Emit CA_EVENT JSON using fmt.Println or t.Log. Build dependencies are offline. Inspect actual source before using internal APIs.","materials":[{"id":"README.md:1:80","file":"README.md","start_line":1,"end_line":80,"kind":"document_statement","text":"raft [![Build Status](https://github.com/hashicorp/raft/workflows/ci/badge.svg)](https://github.com/hashicorp/raft/actions)\n====\n\nraft is a [Go](http://www.golang.org) library that manages a replicated\nlog and can be used with an FSM to manage replicated state machines. It\nis a library for providing [consensus](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consensus_(computer_science)).\n\nThe use cases for such a library are far-reaching, such as replicated state\nmachines which are a key component of many distributed systems. They enable\nbuilding Consistent, Partition Tolerant (CP) systems, with limited\nfault tolerance as well.\n\n## Building\n\nIf you wish to build raft you'll need Go version 1.16+ installed.\n\nPlease check your installation with:\n\n```\ngo version\n```\n\n## Documentation\n\nFor complete documentation, see the associated [Godoc](http://godoc.org/github.com/hashicorp/raft).\n\nTo prevent complications with cgo, the primary backend `MDBStore` is in a separate repository,\ncalled [raft-mdb](http://github.com/hashicorp/raft-mdb). That is the recommended implementation\nfor the `LogStore` and `StableStore`.\n\nA pure Go backend using [Bbolt](https://github.com/etcd-io/bbolt) is also available called\n[raft-boltdb](https://github.com/hashicorp/raft-boltdb). It can also be used as a `LogStore`\nand `StableStore`.\n\n\n## Community Contributed Examples \n- [Raft gRPC Example](https://github.com/Jille/raft-grpc-example) - Utilizing the Raft repository with gRPC\n- [Raft-based KV-store Example](https://github.com/otoolep/hraftd) - Uses Hashicorp Raft to build a distributed key-value store\n\n\n## Tagged Releases\n\nAs of September 2017, HashiCorp will start using tags for this library to clearly indicate\nmajor version updates. We recommend you vendor your application's dependency on this library.\n\n* v0.1.0 is the original stable version of the library that was in main and has been maintained\nwith no breaking API changes. This was in use by Consul prior to version 0.7.0.\n\n* v1.0.0 takes the changes that were staged in the library-v2-stage-one branch. This version\nmanages server identities using a UUID, so introduces some breaking API changes. It also versions\nthe Raft protocol, and requires some special steps when interoperating with Raft servers running\nolder versions of the library (see the detailed comment in config.go about version compatibility).\nYou can reference https://github.com/hashicorp/consul/pull/2222 for an idea of what was required\nto port Consul to these new interfaces.\n\n    This version includes some new features as well, including non voting servers, a new address\n    provider abstraction in the transport layer, and more resilient snapshots.\n\n## Protocol\n\nraft is based on [\"Raft: In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm\"](https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf)\n\nA high level overview of the Raft protocol is described below, but for details please read the full\n[Raft paper](https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf)\nfollowed by the raft source. Any questions about the raft protocol should be sent to the\n[raft-dev mailing list](https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/raft-dev).\n\n### Protocol Description\n\nRaft nodes are always in one of three states: follower, candidate or leader. All\nnodes initially start out as a follower. In this state, nodes can accept log entries\nfrom a leader and cast votes. If no entries are received for some time, nodes\nself-promote to the candidate state. In the candidate state nodes request votes from\ntheir peers. If a candidate receives a quorum of votes, then it is promoted to a leader.\nThe leader must accept new log entries and replicate to all the other followers.\nIn addition, if stale reads are not acceptable, all queries must also be performed on\nthe leader.\n\nOnce a cluster has a leader, it is able to accept new log entries. A client can\nrequest that a leader append a new log entry, which is an opaque binary blob to","content_digest":"04e71e47bb86a2b8a1c8669f00dcd9e2d76599ad5889219f5a6bdaf46b0495c0"},{"id":"docs/README.md:1:80","file":"docs/README.md","start_line":1,"end_line":80,"kind":"document_statement","text":"# Raft Developer Documentation\n\nThis documentation provides a high level introduction to the `hashicorp/raft`\nimplementation. The intended audience is anyone interested in understanding\nor contributing to the code.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [Terminology](#terminology)\n2. [Operations](#operations)\n   1. [Apply](./apply.md)\n3. [Threads](#threads)\n\n\n## Terminology\n\nThis documentation uses the following terms as defined.\n\n* **Cluster** - the set of peers in the raft configuration\n* **Peer** - a node that participates in the consensus protocol using `hashicorp/raft`. A\n  peer may be in one of the following states: **follower**, **candidate**, or **leader**.\n* **Log** - the full set of log entries.\n* **Log Entry** - an entry in the log. Each entry has an index that is used to order it\n  relative to other log entries.\n  * **Committed** -  A log entry is considered committed if it is safe for that entry to be\n    applied to state machines. A log entry is committed once the leader that created the\n    entry has replicated it on a majority of the peers. A peer has successfully\n    replicated the entry once it is persisted.\n  * **Applied** - log entry applied to the state machine (FSM)\n* **Term** - raft divides time into terms of arbitrary length. Terms are numbered with\n  consecutive integers. Each term begins with an election, in which one or more candidates\n  attempt to become leader. If a candidate wins the election, then it serves as leader for\n  the rest of the term. If the election ends with a split vote, the term will end with no\n  leader.\n* **FSM** - finite state machine, stores the cluster state\n* **Client** - the application that uses the `hashicorp/raft` library\n\n## Operations\n\n### Leader Write\n\nMost write operations must be performed on the leader.\n\n* RequestConfigChange - update the raft peer list configuration\n* Apply - apply a log entry to the log on a majority of peers, and the FSM. See [raft apply](apply.md) for more details.\n* Barrier - a special Apply that does not modify the FSM, used to wait for previous logs to be applied\n* LeadershipTransfer - stop accepting client requests, and tell a different peer to start a leadership election\n* Restore (Snapshot) - overwrite the cluster state with the contents of the snapshot (excluding cluster configuration)\n* VerifyLeader - send a heartbeat to all voters to confirm the peer is still the leader\n\n### Follower Write\n\n* BootstrapCluster - store the cluster configuration in the local log store\n\n\n### Read\n\nRead operations can be performed on a peer in any state.\n\n* AppliedIndex - get the index of the last log entry applied to the FSM\n* GetConfiguration - return the latest cluster configuration\n* LastContact - get the last time this peer made contact with the leader\n* LastIndex - get the index of the latest stored log entry\n* Leader - get the address of the peer that is currently the leader\n* Snapshot - snapshot the current state of the FSM into a file\n* State - return the state of the peer\n* Stats - return some stats about the peer and the cluster\n\n## Threads\n\nRaft uses the following threads to handle operations. The name of the thread is in bold,\nand a short description of the operation handled by the thread follows. The main thread is\nresponsible for handling many operations.\n\n* **run** (main thread) - different behaviour based on peer state\n   * follower\n      * processRPC (from rpcCh)\n         * AppendEntries\n         * RequestVote\n         * InstallSnapshot","content_digest":"38b6a75238a17215c4f5c9881124eddb4c1f6e68cfae16b74cbf5756fa64d2bb"}],"audit_spec":{"version":1,"target_profile":{"system_boundary":"Repository implementation of the Raft library, including its built-in storage and transport variants where selected. Application FSM semantics and separately maintained storage backends are outside the concrete implementation currently supplied; library invocation and completion handling remain inside the boundary.","protocol_contexts":["Follower, candidate and leader roles; candidate processing uses election terms and optional pre-vote.","RPC protocol versions and snapshot versions have separate compatibility checks.","Log entries carry index, term and type.","Configuration state distinguishes latest and committed versions by log index.","A commitment object contains a voter map and a leader-term start index."],"external_interfaces":["Apply, ApplyLog, Barrier and VerifyLeader return futures.","Transport exposes RPC delivery, replication pipelines, votes, snapshot transfer and TimeoutNow.","WithPreVote is an optional transport extension.","FSM, BatchingFSM and FSMSnapshot expose application callbacks.","Snapshot storage exposes Create and Open; sinks expose Close, Cancel and ID.","Log storage supplies index lookup and range deletion in the observed snapshot paths."],"ownership":["Candidate event processing runs in the protocol main thread.","commitment serializes voter-map and commit-index access with a mutex.","runFSM owns local application index and term and invokes application callbacks.","takeSnapshot obtains configuration state through a future because the main thread owns it.","FileSnapshotSink owns file finalization, metadata publication and invocation of snapshot retention.","Transport implementations may deliver heartbeats through a separate fast-pass callback."],"selection_injection":["runFSM selects batching and configuration-storage behavior through interface assertions on r.fsm.","Snapshot installation selects whole-log removal when the log store implements MonotonicLogStore and reports IsMonotonic; otherwise it selects trailing-log compaction.","FileSnapshotSink is an observed built-in implementation; its actual selection and noSync configuration are unknown.","Transport permits optional pre-vote and heartbeat handling. Constructor wiring and effective transport selection have not been read.","README identifies separately maintained MDB and Bbolt backends. Their recommendation does not establish the active backend.","File lookup lists in-memory storage, snapshot and transport implementations, a discard snapshot implementation and a log cache; their semantics and activation remain unread."],"variants":["Pre-vote enabled, disabled or bypassed for leadership transfer.","Individual FSM application versus BatchingFSM application.","Optional ConfigurationStore callback.","Versioned snapshot configuration versus legacy peer decoding.","Monotonic log reset versus trailing-log compaction.","File snapshot synchronization enabled or disabled; parent-directory synchronization skipped on Windows.","Legacy and newer membership encodings described in config.go; complete activation rules remain unread."],"source_ids":["README.md:1:80","docs/README.md:1:80","transport.go:1:80","config.go:1:80","commitment.go:1:104","raft.go:285:441","fsm.go:1:80","fsm.go:81:240","raft.go:1814:1955","snapshot.go:125:278","file_snapshot.go:397:500"],"unknowns":["Constructor, startup reconstruction and goroutine wiring have not been inspected.","Selected deployment configuration, transport, log store, stable store and snapshot store are unknown.","Successful storage calls have not yet been tied to concrete crash-persistence guarantees.","Normal replication, leader dispatch and commit-to-FSM handoffs remain unread.","Package-test availability is supplied capability metadata, not evidence of behavioral correctness or deterministic schedule control."]},"activities":[{"class_id":"A1","applicability":"applicable","purpose":"Form and advance decisions from operation proposals and participant support.","realization_summary":"commitment records increasing match indexes for current voters and advances a local aggregate index at a majority order statistic subject to startIndex. The support producers and downstream commit consumer remain unknown.","entry_points":["commitment.match","commitment.recalculate","newCommitment"],"behavior_ids":["B4"],"variants":["Voter-map replacement through setConfiguration","Empty voter map produces no advancement"],"unknowns":["Persistence and context checks before match calls","Leader commit dispatch and follower commit advancement"],"source_ids":["commitment.go:1:104"]},{"class_id":"A2","applicability":"applicable","purpose":"Acquire and change protocol authority and interpret term-scoped work.","realization_summary":"runCandidate tallies pre-votes and votes, requests role transitions, rejects leader-only operations and restarts after timeout. Snapshot installation also changes local role and term for a newer request.","entry_points":["Raft.runCandidate","Raft.checkRPCHeader","Raft.installSnapshot"],"behavior_ids":["B2"],"variants":["Pre-vote","Direct election","Leadership-transfer election"],"unknowns":["Election-result production and uniqueness","Vote persistence","Follower timers and leader lease handling","State-setter sid